2. NEW blocking finding — behaviour numbering is 0-based in the one file
   whose subject is per-behaviour red evidence
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
packages/cli/src/rules/evidence.test.ts numbers its cases 0..5:

  // behaviour 0: identical bodies after header strip are duplicates
  ...
  // behaviour 5: empty body is not a module load failure

Every other test in the house — including packages/cli/src/rules/process.test.ts,
the sibling file in the same directory — numbers from 1, matching the brief's
"## Behaviours to test" list, matching countBehaviours() (evidence.ts:106,
which yields 1..N), and matching the ci/reds/<id>/NN.log contract
(01.log..06.log). Observed:

  $ node --import tsx packages/cli/src/rules/evidence.test.ts . 6
  exit=0            <- no assertion runs at all; brief behaviour 6 is unreachable

  $ node --import tsx packages/cli/src/rules/evidence.test.ts . 1
  PASS  different body = not duplicate                               false
  exit=0            <- this is the brief's behaviour 2, not behaviour 1

Consequences, all inside the opus's own subject matter:
- `bisellium red W-022 --behaviour 6 -- node --import tsx
  packages/cli/src/rules/evidence.test.ts . 6` records exit 0 — a log that
  this opus's own sibling rule (opus.red_evidence, evidence.ts:247) would
  report as "records a passing run, not a red".
- Behaviours 1..5 captured that way are each off by one against the brief.
- Two such captures over a filter that selects nothing would produce
  byte-identical empty bodies — which isDuplicateRed deliberately does not
  flag (bodyA.length > 0 guard, verified: isDuplicateRed("# exit: 1\n",
  "# exit: 1\n") === false). So the silent-miss path stays silent.

Round 1 checked that 6 behaviours pass and that they map one-to-one onto the
brief's list; it did not check that they are *addressable* by the numbers the
reds store uses. The remediation round 1 prescribed ("six `bisellium red
W-022 --behaviour N`" captures) cannot be carried out correctly until this
is fixed. Blocking: the opus mechanizing red-evidence integrity ships a test
file whose per-behaviour handle is misaligned with the red-evidence contract.

3. Round 1's newline note — real, and narrower than feared
-----------------------------------------------------------
MODULE_LOAD_RE (evidence.ts:150) uses `.` without the `s` flag. Probe run
(temporary script, deleted after the run):

  DIFF  wrapped SyntaxError (newline)              got=false want=true
  ok    real node frame + one-line SyntaxError     got=true  want=true
  ok    ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND                       got=true  want=true
  ok    ERR_UNSUPPORTED_DIR_IMPORT                 got=false want=false
  ok    Cannot find package (msg only)             got=false want=false
  ok    tsx/TS compile error                       got=false want=false
  DIFF  assertion line naming the code (self-ref)  got=true  want=false

Round 1's suspicion is confirmed as a true miss, but V8 emits "does not
provide an export named" on a single line; the multi-line frame preceding it
does not defeat the regex (second case above passes). Non-blocking, agreed.

The probe found a second, more reachable inversion round 1 did not name:
isModuleLoadFailure is applied to the WHOLE log text, header included
(evidence.ts:213), so any red whose assertion output merely mentions the
string is flagged. A red captured from evidence.test.ts itself — whose
fixtures contain "ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND" — would advise "module-load failure,
not assertion-level" about a genuine assertion failure. Advisory level, so
non-blocking, but it fires precisely on this opus's own reds.

Also non-blocking, recorded for the class:
- isDuplicateRed is byte-equality after header strip, so it catches copied
  files only. Verified: bodies differing only by a random temp-dir suffix
  compare false (studio/ci/reds/W-021/01.log contains exactly such a suffix;
  studio/ci/reds/W-029/01.log carries wall-clock timestamps). Re-running one
  command per behaviour — the actual failure mode the class describes —
  slips past. Matches the brief as written ("identical bodies reused"), so
  not a defect against spec; worth a P-00x if the class recurs.
- No cross-opus duplicate detection (a red copied from another opus's store
  is invisible). Out of brief scope.
- No examples/fixtures/ fixture for opus.red_content. The house pattern is a
  fixture + EXPECT per blocking rule (packages/cli/src/test.ts), but no red
  rule has one — W-021's opus.red_evidence and opus.untracked have none
  either, so this is a repo-wide gap, not a W-022 regression.
- packages/cli/src/rules/process.ts gained process.review_tier today
  (df762b6). Unrelated to W-022; typecheck and the suite are clean with both
  in the tree; no interaction found.

